# STEMROBIN-182 / chat182-4: Blind FIRST

Offline internal independent solve pass 1. Exactly 1052-1059: 8 exercises,
20 tasks, counts 1,2,4,3,2,4,2,2. ASCII a,b,c,d denote the source's first,
second, third, fourth Cyrillic labels. No comparison is authorized.

Primary variables and parameters are real. Explicit complex extensions below
refer to all polynomial roots with multiplicity, not just the real roots.

## 1052

Vieta gives x1+x2=-p and x1*x2=q, hence
x1^2+x2^2=(x1+x2)^2-2*x1*x2=p^2-2q.
For real p,q the real-root reading needs p^2-4q>=0. The same identity holds
for both complex roots, including repeated roots.

## 1053

a. The sum is 8. Then 3(x1+x2)+x2=29 gives x2=5, x1=3, k=15.

b. The sum is -2/3 and x1=-3*x2/2. Thus x2=4/3, x1=-2, and
k=3*x1*x2=-8.

## 1054

a. Set t=x^2+6x=(x+3)^2-9>=-9.
(t+8)(t-3)=0 gives t=-8 or 3.
The roots are -4, -2, -3-2*sqrt(3), -3+2*sqrt(3).

b. Set t=x^2-2x-5=(x-1)^2-6>=-6.
(t-3)(t+1)=0 gives t=3 or -1.
The roots are -2, 4, 1-sqrt(5), 1+sqrt(5).

c. Set t=x^2+3x-25=(x+3/2)^2-109/4, real when x is real.
The equation becomes (t-1)^2+6=0: no real solutions.
Over C, t=1+delta*i*sqrt(6); the four roots are
(-3+epsilon*sqrt(113+4*delta*i*sqrt(6)))/2,
with epsilon and delta independently +1 or -1.

d. Set t=(y+2)^2>=0. The equation is (t-4)(t+3)=0.
Only t=4 is allowed over R, giving y=-4 or 0.
The additional complex roots are -2+/-i*sqrt(3).

## 1055

All exclusions are imposed before clearing a denominator. Each reciprocal
pair needs both its numerator and denominator nonzero, so t!=0.

a. x!=-7,1,-1. Put t=(x^2-1)/(x+7).
t+1/t=5/2 gives (2t-1)(t-2)=0.
t=2 gives x^2-2x-15=0, roots 5,-3.
t=1/2 gives 2x^2-x-9=0, roots (1+/-sqrt(73))/4.
Neither branch polynomial vanishes at an excluded value.

b. x!=2,0,3. Put t=(x^2-3x)/(x-2).
Again t=2 or 1/2.
t=2 gives x^2-5x+4=0, roots 1,4.
t=1/2 gives 2x^2-7x+2=0, roots (7+/-sqrt(33))/4.
Neither branch polynomial vanishes at an excluded value.

c. y!=2/3,-1. Put t=(y+1)^2/(3y-2).
The source mixed number 3 3/4 is 15/4.
t-1/t=15/4 gives (4t+1)(t-4)=0.
t=4 gives y^2-10y+9=0, roots 1,9.
t=-1/4 gives 4y^2+11y+2=0, roots (-11+/-sqrt(89))/8.
Neither branch polynomial vanishes at an excluded value. Numerator
(y+1)^2 is positive on the allowed domain, so the first branch has
y>2/3 and the second branch has y<2/3.

## 1056

a. Set t=2x^2+7x-8=2(x+7/4)^2-113/8.
t(t+5)-6=(t+6)(t-1)=0.
t=1 gives 2x^2+7x-9=0, roots 1,-9/2.
t=-6 gives 2x^2+7x-2=0, roots (-7+/-sqrt(65))/4.
Both auxiliary values are in the real substitution's range.

b. Preserve the literal equation:

```text
(x^2-10x-2)-2x^2+20x=15
```

It simplifies to -x^2+10x-17=0, equivalently x^2-10x+17=0.
The shift t=x-5 gives t^2=8, so x=5+/-2*sqrt(2).
The displayed source is already quadratic. This is an unresolved source
context alert, not permission to insert an exponent or solve a guessed
higher-degree equation.

## 1057

Set t=x^2 or y^2. In R, t>=0. Opposite pairs have sum zero.

| Task | Auxiliary equation | Real roots | Real sum | Real product | All complex roots | Complex sum | Complex product |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| a | (t-5)(t-10)=0 | +/-sqrt(5), +/-sqrt(10) | 0 | 50 | same four | 0 | 50 |
| b | (t-4)(t+3)=0 | +/-2 | 0 | -4 | +/-2, +/-i*sqrt(3) | 0 | -12 |
| c | (4t-1)(9t-1)=0 | +/-1/2, +/-1/3 | 0 | 1/36 | same four | 0 | 1/36 |
| d | (16t-1)(t+1)=0 | +/-1/4 | 0 | -1/16 | +/-1/4, +/-i | 0 | -1/16 |

In b the quartic's constant term is the product of all four complex roots,
not the product of the two real roots. In d these different multisets happen
to have the same product because the imaginary pair's product is 1.

## 1058

a. x=sqrt(3+sqrt(7)) is real and positive. With z=x^2=3+sqrt(7),
z^2-6z+2=(z-3)^2-7=0.

b. x=sqrt((3+sqrt(5))/2) is real and positive. With
z=x^2=(3+sqrt(5))/2, z^2-3z+1=(z-3/2)^2-5/4=0.

## 1059

The question is about no real roots, with c real; a quartic still has
complex roots for every c.

a. t=x^2>=0. Then t^2-12t+c=(t-6)^2+c-36, whose minimum on
t>=0 is c-36. No real roots exactly when c>36.
For c<=36, t=6+sqrt(36-c)>=0 produces real x.
At c=36, x=+/-sqrt(6), each double.

b. A root requires t=x^2>0 since the constant term is 100. Then
c=t+100/t=20+(t-10)^2/t>=20.
Conversely c>=20 gives positive t=(c+sqrt(c^2-400))/2.
No real roots exactly when c<20.
At c=20, x=+/-sqrt(10), each double.
Even when c<=-20 makes the auxiliary discriminant nonnegative, both
auxiliary roots are negative and give no real x.

